
Part A: Foundation Section


1. Numerical Ability

Format: 20 Questions | 25 Minutes
• Number System: Divisibility rules, LCM & HCF, Decimals, Fractions, and Surds.
• Arithmetic: Percentages, Profit & Loss, Simple & Compound Interest.
	• Averages, Ratios & Proportions, Alligations & Mixtures.
	• Time & Work, Pipes & Cisterns, Speed, Time & Distance.
• Modern Math: Permutations & Combinations, Probability, Clocks & Calendars.
• Geometry & Mensuration: Area and Perimeter of 2D shapes, Surface Area and Volume of 3D shapes.
• Data Interpretation: Pie Charts, Bar Graphs, Line Graphs, and Tabular Data.
• Statistics: Mean, Median, Mode, Variance, and Standard Deviation.

2. Verbal Ability

Format: 25 Questions | 25 Minutes
• Grammar: Subject-Verb Agreement, Tenses, Prepositions, Articles, and Active/Passive Voice.
• Vocabulary: Synonyms, Antonyms, Idioms & Phrases, and Spellings.
• Sentence Structure: Sentence Completion (Cloze Test), Sentence Arrangement (Para Jumbles), and Sentence Improvement.
• Comprehension: Reading passages to identify main ideas, themes, and inferences.

3. Reasoning Ability

Format: 20 Questions | 25 Minutes
• Logical Reasoning: Syllogisms, Blood Relations, Coding-Decoding, and Direction Sense.
• Analytical Reasoning: Linear & Circular Seating Arrangements, Puzzles, and Data Sufficiency.
• Visual Reasoning: Paper Cutting & Folding, Cubes & Dice, and Figure Classification.
• Critical Reasoning: Statement & Conclusion, Statement & Assumptions, and Cause & Effect.

Part B: Advanced Section


1. Advanced Quants & Reasoning

Format: 14 Questions | 25 Minutes
• Context: This section is a higher-difficulty version of the Foundation topics, with a heavy emphasis on:
• Complex Quants: Logarithms, Progressions (AP/GP), Trigonometry, and Coordinate Geometry.
• Advanced Logic: Complex Seating Arrangements and logical connectivity puzzles.

2. Advanced Coding

Format: 2 Questions | 90 Minutes
Allowed Languages: C, C++, Java, Python, or Perl.

Data Structures:

• Arrays: Rotation, Kadane's Algorithm, Two-pointer techniques, and Hashing.
• Strings: Anagrams, Palindromes, Pattern Matching, and String compression.
• Linked Lists & Trees: Basic implementation and traversal (less frequent but possible for Prime).

Algorithms:

• Sorting/Searching: Quick Sort, Merge Sort, and Binary Search.
• Logic-Based: Number Theory (Prime factors, GCD, Armstrong numbers), Greedy Algorithms, and Basic Dynamic Programming (e.g., Fibonacci, Staircase problem).
Would you like to try a practice problem on one of these high-weightage topics like Kadane's Algorithm or Logarithms?

